WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.420
Presidential candidate Michael Bloomberg
is worth a reported 64 billion dollars.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.389
His opponent Bernie Sanders, a
millionaire and self-proclaimed

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:18.449
socialist, calls that wealth "grotesque"
and "immoral". Is it? In 2018, Bloomberg LP

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:22.439
cleared a two billion dollar profit on
ten billion in revenue. So eight billion

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:26.730
mostly went to pay the company's
nineteen thousand employees. By squeezing

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:31.710
billionaires, we risk reducing innovation
and harming workers. Building and

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.640
managing a large business can be
virtuous, and comes with personal rewards —

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.730
and costs. Work becomes life, replacing
hobbies, family, and relationships. The

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.899
real-life scandals on Page Six show that
money can warp personalities and wreck

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:51.510
human happiness. Money is important and
makes our lives easier. But if we could

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.670
see the sacrifices behind the fortunes,
would we still be envious? Adam Smith

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.239
likened large fortunes to a curse.
Wealthy business owners think they're

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.650
working for themselves, he said, but the
payoff mostly winds up in the pockets of

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.640
people they never even met.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.480
Does money buy happiness? Or does it come

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.370
from somewhere else? Let us know in our
poll. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:19.200
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60
seconds, and check the link in the

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:26.150
description below for my new piece on
Sanders, Bloomberg, and Adam Smith.

